**Burnside's lemma.** Let a finite group $G$ act on a set $X$ (finite, or at least with finitely many fixed points for each $g$). Count $\mathcal F=\{(g,x)\in G\times X: gx=x\}$ in two ways:
$$|\mathcal F|=\sum_{g\in G}|\operatorname{Fix}(g)|=\sum_{x\in X}|\operatorname{Stab}(x)|=\sum_{x\in X}\frac{|G|}{|Gx|}$$
by orbit–stabilizer. Grouping the $x$ by orbit, each orbit $O$ contributes $\sum_{x\in O}\frac{|G|}{|O|}=|G|$. Hence $\sum_g|\operatorname{Fix}(g)|=|G|\cdot\#\{\text{orbits}\}$, i.e.
$$\#\{\text{orbits}\}=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}|\operatorname{Fix}(g)| .$$

**Colorings of the corners.** The symmetry group of the square is the dihedral group of order $8$ acting on the $10^4$ colorings. A coloring is fixed by $g$ iff it is constant on each cycle of $g$ on the corners, so $|\operatorname{Fix}(g)|=10^{\#\text{cycles}}$:

- identity: $4$ cycles, $10^4$;
- rotations by $90^\circ$, $270^\circ$: $1$ cycle each, $10$ each;
- rotation by $180^\circ$: $2$ cycles, $10^2$;
- the two reflections in diagonals: fix $2$ corners, swap the other $2$: $3$ cycles, $10^3$ each;
- the two reflections in lines through midpoints of opposite sides: $2$ cycles, $10^2$ each.

$$\frac{10000+20+100+2000+200}{8}=\frac{12320}{8}=1540 .$$

**Answer:** $1540$ colorings up to symmetry (if only rotations were allowed, the count would be $\frac{10000+100+20}{4}=2530$). ∎
